[16.045459, "i", "cat > ~/terminal-bench-recipe.yaml << 'EOF'\ndescription: terminal-bench recipe\ninstructions: You are given a task and you need to complete it. You are currently\n  executing in a docker container where you are being evaluated on a benchmark for\n  LLM agents. Act autonomously. You will not receive any feedback on your progress,\n  so you must use your own tools to complete the task without any intervention.\nprompt: \"Train a LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adapter for a Hugging Face language model\\\n  \\ to perform biomedical question answering.\\n\\nYou are provided with a biomedical\\\n  \\ multiple-choice QA dataset in the /app/data directory containing:\\n- train.json:\\\n  \\ 300 training samples\\n- val.json: 100 validation samples\\n\\nNOTE: The test set\\\n  \\ is NOT available in the data folder during training to prevent overfitting. \\n\\\n  It is stored separately and only accessible during the evaluation phase.\\n\\nEach\\\n  \\ sample has the following structure:\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"What is the primary\\\n  \\ function of hemoglo"]
[16.045545, "i", "bin?\\\",\\n  \\\"options\\\": [\\\"A) Transport oxygen\\\", \\\"B) Fight\\\n  \\ infections\\\", \\\"C) Clot blood\\\", \\\"D) Digest food\\\"],  // Exactly 4 options (A,\\\n  \\ B, C, D)\\n  \\\"answer\\\": \\\"A\\\"\\n}\\n\\nRequirements:\\n1. Use the openai-community/gpt2\\\n  \\ model as the base model for fine-tuning\\n2. Implement LoRA fine-tuning with the\\\n  \\ following configuration:\\n   - LoRA rank (r): 8\\n   - LoRA alpha: 16\\n   - Target\\\n  \\ modules: ['c_attn'] (attention layers)\\n3. Train the model using the provided\\\n  \\ training and validation sets\\n4. Save your training script to /app/train_lora.py\\\n  \\ for verification\\n5. Save the trained LoRA adapter to /app/experiments/lora_adapter,\\\n  \\ including:\\n   - adapter_config.json (LoRA configuration)\\n   - adapter_model.safetensors\\\n  \\ (LoRA weights)\\n   - tokenizer_config.json (tokenizer configuration)\\n   - adapter_config\\\n  \\ should include default arguments including 'base_model_name_or_path' and 'task_type'\\\n  \\ \\n6. It is recommended to use the PEFT (peft==0.15.0) library for impleme"]
[16.046466, "o", "cat > ~/terminal-bench-recipe.yaml << 'EOF'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[16.047063, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> description: terminal-bench recipe\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> instructions: You are given a task and you need to complete it. You are currently\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   executing in a docker container where you are being evaluated on a benchmark for\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   LLM agents. Act autonomously. You will not receive any feedback on your progress,\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   so you must use your own tools to complete the task without any intervention.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[16.047071, "i", "nting\\\n  \\ LoRA fine-tuning.\\n7. Create a results file at /app/lora_results.json with the\\\n  \\ exact schema below\\n\\nRequired /app/lora_results.json schema:\\n{\\n  \\\"model\\\"\\\n  : string,           // Base model name used (e.g., \\\"openai-community/gpt2\\\")\\n\\\n  \\  \\\"lora_config\\\": {\\n    \\\"r\\\": number,            // LoRA rank (must be 8)\\n\\\n  \\    \\\"alpha\\\": number,        // LoRA alpha (must be 16)\\n    \\\"epochs\\\": number\\\n  \\        // Number of training epochs\\n  },\\n  \\\"training_samples\\\": number,   \\\n  \\  // Number of training samples used\\n  \\\"validation_samples\\\": number    // Number\\\n  \\ of validation samples used\\n}\\n\\nExpected outputs:\\n- /app/train_lora.py: Your\\\n  \\ training script\\n- /app/experiments/lora_adapter/: Directory containing the LoRA\\\n  \\ adapter weights and configuration, and can be loaded to generate responses\\n-\\\n  \\ /app/lora_results.json: JSON file matching the schema above\\n\\nPerformance target:\\\n  \\ The fine-tuned model should achieve >35% accuracy on the held-out biomedical QA\\\n  "]
[16.047076, "i", "\\ test set.\"\ntitle: terminal-bench\nversion: 1.0.0\nEOF\r"]
